.TH WMTOUCHD 1 "2026" "Window Maker"
.SH NAME
wmtouchd \- Window Maker touchpad gesture daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmtouchd
[\fB\-\-seat\fR\ \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR\ \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-socket\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B wmtouchd
\fB\-\-source-events\fR\ \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-\-config\fR\ \fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B wmtouchd
\fB\-\-check\fR [\fB\-\-config\fR\ \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-socket\fR\ \fIPATH\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wmtouchd
reads three- to five-finger touchpad swipes and two- to five-finger
pinches through libinput and turns them into Window Maker actions. It
links libinput, libudev and libWUtil, with no X11 dependency, and talks
to the window manager over the same AF_UNIX socket used by
.BR wmakermsg (1).
.PP
Each recognised gesture is delivered as the generic
.B action
IPC verb, so any action a key binding can trigger is reachable without
teaching Window Maker anything about gestures. The gesture-to-action
mapping lives in the
.B WMTouch
defaults domain, never in the
.B WindowMaker
domain. That domain maps gesture names to action names, for example:
.PP
.nf
{
    SwipeThreshold      = 60;
    PinchThreshold      = 1.5;
    ThreeFingerLeft     = "PrevWorkspace";
    ThreeFingerRight    = "NextWorkspace";
    ThreeFingerUp       = "ShowDesktop";
    ThreeFingerDown     = "None";
    ThreeFingerPinchOut = "Maximize";
    ThreeFingerPinchIn  = "Miniaturize";
}
.fi
.PP
The action names are the key-binding action names shown by
.B "wmakermsg info"
(the config key without the trailing
.IR Key ,
for example
.BR NextWorkspace ).
A value of
.BR None ,
empty, or an absent key makes the gesture inert, so the daemon does
nothing until the domain opts in.
.PP
A gesture may also be bound to a chain of actions, given as an array
that is run in order as one batched IPC command, for example:
.PP
.nf
    FourFingerRight = (MoveToNextWorkspace, NextWorkspace);
.fi
.PP
which moves the focused window to the next workspace and follows it.
A chain holds at most 16 actions; entries that are
.BR None ,
empty, or not strings are skipped, and a chain that reduces to nothing
is inert. If an action in the chain fails the rest still run, and each
failed name is logged once.
.PP
Swipe deltas are in libinput gesture units, not screen pixels, and the
unaccelerated accessors are used so
.B SwipeThreshold
does not depend on pointer speed. Deltas follow screen coordinates:
positive
.B dx
is a swipe to the right and positive
.B dy
is a swipe toward the bottom edge of the touchpad, so an upward swipe
reports negative
.BR dy .
.B SwipeAxisMargin
(default 1.2) requires the dominant axis to exceed the other by that
factor, otherwise the gesture fires nothing. The decision is made when
the swipe ends, and a cancelled swipe is dropped.
.PP
A pinch is matched by its scale, the ratio of the current finger distance
to the distance when the gesture began. When the pinch ends, a scale at or
above
.B PinchThreshold
(default 1.5) fires the
.B PinchOut
direction, and a scale at or below its reciprocal fires
.BR PinchIn .
The action keys follow the same finger prefix and direction suffix, for
example
.B ThreeFingerPinchOut
and
.BR TwoFingerPinchIn ,
so each finger count is bound separately. A cancelled pinch is dropped.
The rotation angle libinput reports alongside a pinch is ignored, so a
twist that keeps the finger distance constant fires nothing.
.PP
Reads are non-exclusive: the X pointer driver keeps working alongside
the daemon, and nothing needs to be restored on exit.
.PP
If the Window Maker IPC socket is not present when
.B wmtouchd
starts, or the connection is dropped or refused, it retries with
exponential backoff and gives up after 5 minutes, exiting with status 1.
While disconnected, swipes are still recognised but dropped;
.B \-\-verbose
logs the connection attempts and each dropped action.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Read the configured action names and the window manager's action list
over IPC, print
.B OK
or
.B unknown
for each row, and exit. It never executes an action, so validating a
.B LockScreen
binding does not lock the screen. Exits non-zero when a name is unknown
or the window manager is unreachable.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Load the
.B WMTouch
domain from
.I FILE
instead of the resolved defaults path.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print runtime diagnostics to standard error. Same as
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.BI \-\-device " PATH"
Watch a single input device given as a
.I /dev/input/eventN
path instead of the seat's udev devices. This mode does not follow
hotplug.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-seat " NAME"
Watch the libinput seat
.I NAME
instead of
.BR seat0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " PATH"
Connect to
.I PATH
instead of the resolved IPC socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-source-events " FILE"
Replay newline-separated gesture records from
.IR FILE
once instead of reading a device, then exit. A swipe is
.BI "begin " N \fR,
.BI "update " "N dx dy" \fR,
.BI "end " N
or
.BI "cancel " N \fR,
and a pinch is
.BI "pinch-begin " N \fR,
.BI "pinch-update " "N scale" \fR,
.BI "pinch-end " N
or
.BI "pinch-cancel " N \fR.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored. This mode needs neither a seat nor input-device
permissions.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Print runtime diagnostics to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Reading input devices normally requires one of:
.IP \(bu 2
a logind-managed session, where systemd applies a uaccess ACL to the
active seat's devices and a daemon autostarted from the session inherits
it;
.IP \(bu 2
membership in the
.B input
group;
.IP \(bu 2
running as
.BR root .
.PP
If
.BR getfacl (1)
shows no user entry on the touchpad's
.I /dev/input/eventN
node even though the session is the active one, logind is not applying
uaccess ACLs on this setup. Prefer a device-scoped udev rule over the
.B input
group, which exposes every device, for example in
.IR /etc/udev/rules.d/99-wmtouchd.rules :
.PP
.nf
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{idVendor}=="05ac",
ATTRS{idProduct}=="0324", OWNER="\fIuser\fR", MODE="0600"
.fi
.PP
Apply it with
.B "udevadm control --reload"
and
.BR "udevadm trigger" ,
or by replugging the device. Running under
.BR sudo (8)
also works, but its default env_reset strips the variables that locate
the IPC socket, so pass
.B WMAKER_IPC_SOCKET
after
.B sudo
or use
.BR "sudo -E" .
.PP
No polkit or D-Bus dependency is needed: the daemon does not monitor
kernel uevents, hotplug is handled by re-polling the libinput file
descriptor.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the
.B WMTouch
domain from disk.
.TP
.B SIGTERM , SIGINT
Exit cleanly with status 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean exit on
.BR SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGINT ,
or after a
.B \-\-source-events
replay.
.TP
.B 1
Startup failure, an unknown action in
.BR \-\-check ,
or give-up after 5 minutes without a usable IPC socket.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/WindowMaker/WMTouch
XDG location for the
.B WMTouch
defaults domain.
.TP
.I ~/GNUstep/Defaults/WMTouch
Legacy location for the
.B WMTouch
defaults domain.
.SH NOTES
Swipes are recognised for three to five fingers: libinput classifies
two-finger touchpad motion as scrolling, so pinch gestures are the only
ones that start at two fingers. Rotate and hold gestures are ignored.
.B \-\-verbose
logs their finger count. Both a swipe and a pinch are resolved when the
gesture ends, which is the only mode in this version.
.PP
In udev mode the libinput backend reports device add and remove as
events during normal dispatch, so a USB touchpad added after startup is
picked up without restarting the daemon.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR wmaker (1),
.BR wmakermsg (1),
.BR wdwrite (1)
